Aortic Stenosis
Cardiovascular
Ejection systolic murmur radiating to carotids, Slow-rising carotid pulse, Exertional syncope/angina

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-003
Reason for Visit: Episode of exertional presyncope and new-onset chest tightness requiring urgent assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a recent episode of exertional near-fainting accompanied by chest discomfort. This triad in an elderly patient carries a high index of suspicion for critical structural heart disease or acute coronary syndrome. Assessment could not be deferred until the next in-hours period due to the significant risk of sudden cardiac death or acute myocardial infarction, necessitating urgent in-person physical evaluation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I walked out to the letterbox and suddenly felt dizzy, my chest felt tight, and I thought I was going to black out."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports progressive exertional dyspnea over the past three months, now culminating in exertional presyncope today. Accompanied by transient exertional angina pectoris that resolved after 10 minutes of rest. Denies palpitations prior to the dizzy spell. KEY SYMPTOMS: Exertional syncope or presyncope. Exertional angina pectoris. Progressive exertional dyspnoea indicating left ventricular failure.
Functional Status: Previously independent, now restricting mobility out of fear of fainting.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk due to presyncopal episodes; adult child staying overnight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/85 | HR: 68 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 96 percent | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Frail appearing, currently resting comfortably in a chair, no acute respiratory distress at rest.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Harsh ejection systolic murmur loudest in the aortic area, radiating prominently to the carotid arteries. Slow-rising and small-volume carotid pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus). Narrowed pulse pressure. Soft aortic component of the second heart sound. Sustained apical impulse due to left ventricular hypertrophy. Fourth heart sound present. Respiratory: Lungs clear to auscultation bilaterally. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Ejection systolic murmur radiating to carotids, Slow-rising carotid pulse, Exertional syncope/angina (observed by history).
Environmental Observations: Trip hazards removed by family, clear access to the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against physical pill bottles. No active vasodilators prescribed.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Aortic Stenosis - Status: Exacerbated (Symptomatic)
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Chest pain was strictly exertional, transient, resolved with rest, and dominated by typical mechanical murmur findings.

Vasovagal Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear exertional trigger, presence of angina, and profound structural murmur with slow-rising pulse.

Arrhythmia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Regular pulse rate without deficit, no history of sudden palpitations without exertion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a classic triad of exertional presyncope, angina, and dyspnea, which are cardinal signs of symptomatic, potentially critical aortic stenosis. Given the high risk of sudden cardiac death associated with symptomatic severe aortic stenosis, an urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to exclude acute myocardial infarction, evaluate baseline hemodynamic stability, and dictate safe mobilization limits. This urgent clinical necessity precluded delaying care until the next available in-hours appointment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed strict rest. Arranged urgent outpatient transthoracic echocardiography to assess valve leaflet excursion and transvalvular peak velocity gradient.
Positive Behavioral Change: Patient instructed to pace all physical activities by utilizing a chair in the bathroom and kitchen. By minimizing orthostatic and exertional demand, the patient actively reduces ventricular strain. The immediate reward is the absence of dizziness, reinforcing safe, measured movement.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: Shower chair recommended.
Follow-up: Urgent General Practice review on Monday morning to review echocardiogram and refer to cardiology.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If chest pain recurs at rest and lasts longer than 15 minutes, or if complete loss of consciousness occurs, the family is instructed to call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending definitive echocardiographic imaging; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Symptomatic aortic stenosis is a progressively restricting condition. Urgent cardiology review is expected for potential valve replacement consideration.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not perform any strenuous activity. Move slowly from sitting to standing. Seek immediate emergency care for resting chest pain or fainting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The main valve letting blood out of your heart has become stiff and narrow. When you exert yourself, not enough blood reaches your brain, causing dizziness.
Actionable Steps: Rest completely this weekend. Sit down immediately if you feel lightheaded.

